WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:01.368
- [Kelly] Your shoes are amazing.

00:00:01.368 --> 00:00:02.768
Your pearls.

00:00:02.768 --> 00:00:03.601
- All of it.

00:00:03.601 --> 00:00:06.130
- Ah, well meet Bowie, I mean, Rachel.

00:00:06.130 --> 00:00:08.470
- So nice to meet you, I'm
a big fan of both of you.

00:00:08.470 --> 00:00:09.710
- Oh, thank you.

00:00:09.710 --> 00:00:10.640
Give me your baby.

00:00:10.640 --> 00:00:12.109
- Give me your- (Kelly laughs)

00:00:12.109 --> 00:00:13.740
I know it's such a creepy storyline.

00:00:13.740 --> 00:00:16.078
- I re-watched that song in preparation.

00:00:16.078 --> 00:00:17.870
It's just him stealing a baby.

00:00:17.870 --> 00:00:18.703
Go on, this is your segment.

00:00:18.703 --> 00:00:19.536
- Yeah,

00:00:19.536 --> 00:00:20.369
it is a weird thing,

00:00:20.369 --> 00:00:21.469
but I'm still into it.

00:00:21.469 --> 00:00:23.429
So, are there any particular like looks

00:00:23.429 --> 00:00:26.150
from the 80s that you miss or loved?

00:00:26.150 --> 00:00:28.160
- I miss the big hair and
the big shoulder pads.

00:00:28.160 --> 00:00:29.430
- Oh! Brought to you by, what?

00:00:29.430 --> 00:00:30.720
- Oh yeah, absolutely.

00:00:30.720 --> 00:00:31.710
- I love a shoulder pad.

00:00:31.710 --> 00:00:32.690
- I do too, because I mean

00:00:32.690 --> 00:00:33.523
kind of the proportion thing.

00:00:33.523 --> 00:00:34.356
- [Kelly] You knew it.

00:00:34.356 --> 00:00:35.189
- It works for ya.

00:00:35.189 --> 00:00:36.022
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:00:36.022 --> 00:00:37.181
- The big hair, you know,
closer to God. All of it.

00:00:37.181 --> 00:00:38.014
- Amen.

00:00:38.014 --> 00:00:40.270
So, your new show is all about pop culture

00:00:40.270 --> 00:00:41.200
collections right?

00:00:41.200 --> 00:00:42.033
- Right.

00:00:42.033 --> 00:00:43.188
- [Kelly] So, what got you into this show?

00:00:43.188 --> 00:00:44.021
It's interesting.

00:00:44.021 --> 00:00:47.300
- You know, I had filmed a
little pilot with my daughter

00:00:47.300 --> 00:00:48.860
and the producer, that didn't go,

00:00:48.860 --> 00:00:50.377
but the producers called me and said,

00:00:50.377 --> 00:00:51.210
"We've got this show.

00:00:51.210 --> 00:00:52.710
You kind of travel around,

00:00:52.710 --> 00:00:54.350
meeting people with great collections."

00:00:54.350 --> 00:00:55.183
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:00:55.183 --> 00:00:57.640
- And I had just become a life coach,

00:00:57.640 --> 00:00:59.390
and so I'm really fascinated by people,

00:00:59.390 --> 00:01:01.453
especially really people that
are kind of on the fringe

00:01:01.453 --> 00:01:04.350
of you know, living in reality,

00:01:04.350 --> 00:01:05.301
not that I know...

00:01:05.301 --> 00:01:06.543
- On the fringe!

00:01:08.435 --> 00:01:10.223
Look at-- (Kelly laughs)

00:01:10.223 --> 00:01:11.056
(audience laughs)

00:01:11.056 --> 00:01:12.924
- What do you mean?

00:01:12.924 --> 00:01:14.253
Voodoo, you do.

00:01:15.510 --> 00:01:16.790
So I love that they're on the line.

00:01:16.790 --> 00:01:17.820
They're walking a very narrow line.

00:01:17.820 --> 00:01:18.720
- They are, but they're not.

00:01:18.720 --> 00:01:19.737
I mean people often ask me,

00:01:19.737 --> 00:01:20.940
"Are these people hoarders?"

00:01:20.940 --> 00:01:21.800
And they're not,

00:01:21.800 --> 00:01:23.180
because they're very intentional

00:01:23.180 --> 00:01:25.610
and they know their passion,
they know their purpose

00:01:25.610 --> 00:01:28.530
and they are absolutely
100% committed to it.

00:01:28.530 --> 00:01:29.363
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:01:29.363 --> 00:01:31.446
- And it 99% of the
time has something to do

00:01:31.446 --> 00:01:34.485
with trying to hang on to
something from their childhood.

00:01:34.485 --> 00:01:35.318
- [Kelly] Yeah, it's like nostalgia.

00:01:35.318 --> 00:01:36.880
- And I think it's why that,

00:01:36.880 --> 00:01:38.269
you know, why we're having 80s week.

00:01:38.269 --> 00:01:39.102
- Yeah.

00:01:39.102 --> 00:01:39.993
- It's why it's on MeTV.

00:01:39.993 --> 00:01:40.826
- It's why not?

00:01:40.826 --> 00:01:42.075
- We all want to have

00:01:42.075 --> 00:01:44.454
you know a simpler, pure, sweeter time,

00:01:44.454 --> 00:01:46.260
even if it's just 30 minutes.

00:01:46.260 --> 00:01:48.657
- Amen. It's been a complicated few years.

00:01:48.657 --> 00:01:51.030
So did you collect anything growing up

00:01:51.030 --> 00:01:53.120
other than crystal balls?

00:01:53.120 --> 00:01:54.867
- I weirdly I collected snow globes.

00:01:54.867 --> 00:01:55.700
- [Kelly] Oh, so it's
all coming full circle.

00:01:55.700 --> 00:01:58.410
- Which is why this is quite apt.

00:01:58.410 --> 00:01:59.617
- That's amazing. Wait, snow globes, like,

00:01:59.617 --> 00:02:01.950
I used to be obsessed with
those when I was little.

00:02:01.950 --> 00:02:03.830
- Everywhere I went I would
get a different snow globe,

00:02:03.830 --> 00:02:06.740
and I still have one that my
friend sent me from New York

00:02:06.740 --> 00:02:09.850
because he saw a bunch of
cool Broadway shows in 2004.

00:02:09.850 --> 00:02:12.846
And then I have a snow globe
that celebrated the year 2000,

00:02:12.846 --> 00:02:16.497
that if you play it, it
still plays Auld Lang Syne,

00:02:16.497 --> 00:02:18.420
and it has like a cheap little hat

00:02:18.420 --> 00:02:20.630
and bottle of champagne in the center.

00:02:20.630 --> 00:02:21.463
I'm just gonna keep doing this.

00:02:21.463 --> 00:02:22.296
- It would've been an
amazing snow globe, though,

00:02:22.296 --> 00:02:23.260
remember when everybody thought everyone

00:02:23.260 --> 00:02:24.690
was gonna die in 2000?

00:02:24.690 --> 00:02:25.878
It would've been funny if it just was like

00:02:25.878 --> 00:02:27.763
a self-destructing snow globe.

00:02:28.606 --> 00:02:31.230
- If it's just an
apocalyptic snow globe? Man.

00:02:31.230 --> 00:02:32.440
- Would've been a missed opportunity.

00:02:32.440 --> 00:02:34.190
- And then it does nothing, though,

00:02:34.190 --> 00:02:35.740
because that's what happened at Y2K.

00:02:35.740 --> 00:02:36.690
It did nothing.

00:02:36.690 --> 00:02:37.770
- I know!

00:02:37.770 --> 00:02:39.017
Everybody was so, they were like,

00:02:39.017 --> 00:02:41.010
everybody was storing rice.

00:02:41.010 --> 00:02:42.210
I'm like, okay.

00:02:42.210 --> 00:02:44.260
But I mean, it's just
like a weird... anyway.

00:02:44.260 --> 00:02:46.090
And then toilet paper, when COVID hit.

00:02:46.090 --> 00:02:47.297
I'm like, really?

00:02:47.297 --> 00:02:50.110
Like, out of all the things
you could be collecting

00:02:50.110 --> 00:02:51.640
in case (beep) hits the fan?

00:02:51.640 --> 00:02:52.473
I mean, I guess you have
toilet paper for it.

00:02:52.473 --> 00:02:53.306
- Literally.

00:02:53.306 --> 00:02:54.139
- [Lisa] There you go.

00:02:54.139 --> 00:02:54.972
- Yeah, and I was just like,

00:02:54.972 --> 00:02:56.100
I don't understand why
it was toilet paper.

00:02:56.100 --> 00:02:59.180
Anyway, I'm letting go of it, obviously.

00:02:59.180 --> 00:03:00.900
So you're a grandmother now.

00:03:00.900 --> 00:03:02.870
So is that way more fun than being a mom,

00:03:02.870 --> 00:03:03.830
'cause it's just kind of cooler?

00:03:03.830 --> 00:03:06.361
- It is so much more
fun for so many reasons.

00:03:06.361 --> 00:03:07.194
- [Kelly] Let me hear about that.

00:03:07.194 --> 00:03:09.700
- [Lisa] I babysit my
grandkids every Monday, yes.

00:03:09.700 --> 00:03:10.710
- [Kelly] Ahh, look at that little nugget.

00:03:10.710 --> 00:03:12.853
- [Lisa] I know. Echo and Eisley.

00:03:12.853 --> 00:03:13.686
- Echo?

00:03:13.686 --> 00:03:14.519
- Echo.

00:03:14.519 --> 00:03:15.352
- That is so cool.

00:03:15.352 --> 00:03:16.185
- Yeah, they're cool kids.

00:03:16.185 --> 00:03:17.738
Like, I have cool kids and cool grandkids.

00:03:17.738 --> 00:03:18.571
- [Kelly] I love that.

00:03:18.571 --> 00:03:19.404
- I love it, yes.

00:03:19.404 --> 00:03:20.880
- It's just so, I always
hear everybody talking about

00:03:20.880 --> 00:03:23.330
how that's, like, their
favorite thing to be a grandma.

00:03:23.330 --> 00:03:24.470
What do they call you?

00:03:24.470 --> 00:03:26.100
- They call me Coco Mama.

00:03:26.100 --> 00:03:26.933
- [Kelly] What?

00:03:26.933 --> 00:03:27.766
- Yes. Yes.

00:03:27.766 --> 00:03:29.300
- Do you give 'em hot cocoa a lot or?

00:03:29.300 --> 00:03:31.600
- No, no. I mean, this is a
family show, right? So, okay.

00:03:31.600 --> 00:03:32.570
- Yes. Go for it.

00:03:32.570 --> 00:03:34.290
- Well they can't, they'll
never be allowed to watch

00:03:34.290 --> 00:03:35.703
this episode until they're teenagers

00:03:35.703 --> 00:03:38.090
because the name Coco Mama came

00:03:38.090 --> 00:03:40.150
because when their parents got engaged,

00:03:40.150 --> 00:03:41.600
they got engaged in Paris.

00:03:41.600 --> 00:03:43.280
So I'd taken my adult kids.

00:03:43.280 --> 00:03:46.392
We went backpacking across
Europe, stayed in hostels.

00:03:46.392 --> 00:03:47.225
- [Kelly] Wow.

00:03:47.225 --> 00:03:48.400
- So we stopped in Amsterdam.

00:03:48.400 --> 00:03:49.233
- Oh, yes.

00:03:49.233 --> 00:03:52.400
- And we stayed at a
hostel called Coco Mama.

00:03:52.400 --> 00:03:54.928
You know, we loved it, but
we went to a coffee shop.

00:03:54.928 --> 00:03:55.780
- [Kelly] Yes.

00:03:55.780 --> 00:03:58.082
- And they're all adults.

00:03:58.082 --> 00:03:58.915
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:03:58.915 --> 00:04:00.690
- And we decided when in Amsterdam.

00:04:00.690 --> 00:04:01.523
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:04:01.523 --> 00:04:02.356
- You know?

00:04:02.356 --> 00:04:03.189
- Yeah.

00:04:03.189 --> 00:04:04.315
- Well, we did the newbie doobie mistake.

00:04:04.315 --> 00:04:06.057
- [Kelly] It hits you hard.

00:04:06.057 --> 00:04:08.250
- And we thought, "Well
this isn't doing anything,

00:04:08.250 --> 00:04:09.440
let's have another slice."

00:04:09.440 --> 00:04:13.240
- Yeah. Oh, no. Oh my God. Oh.

00:04:13.240 --> 00:04:14.073
- Oh yeah.

00:04:14.073 --> 00:04:15.220
So we're going through the streets

00:04:15.220 --> 00:04:16.627
when it hits me and I'm thinking,

00:04:16.627 --> 00:04:18.737
"Okay, those men over there

00:04:18.737 --> 00:04:22.010
are going to harvest our organs."

00:04:22.010 --> 00:04:23.530
- And everyone's on bikes, riding around

00:04:23.530 --> 00:04:24.458
and you're like dodging 'em.

00:04:24.458 --> 00:04:25.291
- Oh, yes.

00:04:25.291 --> 00:04:26.124
No, I'm stopping them, because I'm saying,

00:04:26.124 --> 00:04:28.203
I'm stopping people on
the street and saying,

00:04:28.203 --> 00:04:30.654
"We're from America. We
just had some space cake.

00:04:30.654 --> 00:04:33.851
I am responsible for these
children. I am not responsible.

00:04:33.851 --> 00:04:36.510
Call an ambulance, call a taxi, whatever."

00:04:36.510 --> 00:04:37.343
- Call God.

00:04:37.343 --> 00:04:38.517
- "Get us to Coco Mama."

00:04:39.382 --> 00:04:43.289
My son-in-law was
sobered up enough to say,

00:04:43.289 --> 00:04:45.870
"Mrs. Cauble," 'cause that's
what he called me then,

00:04:45.870 --> 00:04:46.703
before they got married,

00:04:46.703 --> 00:04:48.699
"I'll get you back to Coco Mama."

00:04:48.699 --> 00:04:52.100
So two days later, we were
in Paris, they got engaged.

00:04:52.100 --> 00:04:54.160
And I said, "Don't call
me Mrs. Cauble anymore,

00:04:54.160 --> 00:04:56.250
You don't have to call
me mom, but you know,

00:04:56.250 --> 00:04:57.720
find something else to call me."

00:04:57.720 --> 00:04:58.553
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:04:58.553 --> 00:05:00.497
- And he said, "I'm gonna
call you Coco Mama."

00:05:01.610 --> 00:05:05.320
- Because you think that
you probably said it once,

00:05:05.320 --> 00:05:07.334
but you probably said it a billion times.

00:05:07.334 --> 00:05:08.275
- A billion times.

00:05:08.275 --> 00:05:09.275
- [Kelly] Coco Mama!

